# How to Use This Document

## What this topic covers

Topic 2 is syllabus **Head III — Feminist Theories and Practice**. The syllabus lists seven lines: **what is feminism**, and six schools — **liberal**, **radical**, **Marxist/socialist**, **psychoanalytic**, **men's** and **postmodern** feminism.

The examiner has also asked about something the syllabus does not name: **intersectionality** — how gender overlaps with class, race, religion and other identities (2025, and applied to Pakistan in 2026). The recent MCQs are full of newer ideas too — **standpoint theory**, **strategic essentialism**, **kyriarchy**, the **matrix of domination**, the **patriarchal bargain**. So this document also teaches **Black**, **postcolonial**, **eco-** and **Muslim** feminism.

In the descriptive paper Topic 2 is the lightest topic: ==7 visits== in eleven papers. But it is the **second-richest source of MCQs** (26 of 140), and its theories are used inside every other topic. When you explain honour killing (Topic 7), quotas (Topic 6) or development (Topic 4), you will reach for a school of feminism from this document.

## How this document is built

1. **Part Zero** gives you the words of the topic, the family tree of feminism, and the four questions every school answers.
2. **Part One** has nine chapters: one on what feminism is, one on each of the six syllabus schools, one on the newer feminisms, and one comparing them all and asking which suits Pakistan.
3. **Part Two** sets out today's debates.
4. **Part Three** lists every past question, what the record shows, and five likely questions.
5. **Part Four** is a bank of about 120 numbered one-liners in the style of the past MCQs, followed by every past MCQ on the topic.
6. **Part Five** is the revision sheet — the whole topic in a few pages, every word explained, for the night before the exam.

::: simple
Every school of feminism agrees that **women are disadvantaged**. They disagree about **why**, **where** the problem lies, and **what to do** about it. Learn each school by its answers to those questions and you will never mix them up.
:::

# Part Zero — The ABC of Topic 2

## 0.1  The words of this topic

::: table | The key terms of Topic 2 (★ = you must be able to define it in one sentence).
@widths 3.3,10.7
Term | Simple meaning, with an example
Feminism ★ | The belief that women and men should have **equal rights and chances**, and the **movement** to achieve it.
Patriarchy ★ | A system in which **men hold most power** and this is treated as natural.
Sexism | Unfair treatment or prejudice because of a person's sex. *Paying a woman less for the same job.*
Liberal feminism ★ | Equality through **equal rights, laws, education and opportunity** within the existing system.
Radical feminism ★ | Patriarchy is the **deepest root** of women's oppression; society must be changed **at the root**.
Marxist feminism ★ | Women's oppression comes from **capitalism and private property**.
Socialist feminism ★ | **Capitalism and patriarchy together** oppress women — the "dual systems" view.
Psychoanalytic feminism ★ | Women's oppression is rooted in **early childhood and the unconscious mind**.
Men's feminism ★ | **Men who support** feminist goals and study how patriarchy shapes men.
Postmodern feminism ★ | There is **no single "woman"** and no single truth; identities are made in **language**.
Post-structural feminism | Close to postmodern: identities are **unstable and made through discourse** (MCQ 2018, 2023).
Intersectionality ★ | Gender **overlaps** with race, class, religion and other identities to create unique disadvantage (Crenshaw, 1989).
Black feminism | Feminism centred on Black women facing **race, class and gender together**.
Postcolonial feminism | Criticises Western feminism for **speaking for "Third World women"**.
Ecofeminism | Links the **domination of women** with the **domination of nature**.
Muslim / Islamic feminism | Gender justice through **re-reading Islamic sources**.
Standpoint theory ★ | **Knowledge depends on social position**; the oppressed can see what the powerful miss.
Consciousness-raising | Small groups where women shared experiences and saw them as **political, not personal**.
"The personal is political" ★ | Private life — marriage, housework, sex — is shaped by **power** too.
Domestic labour | **Unpaid housework and care**; a key subject of Marxist and socialist feminism.
Dual-systems theory | Socialist view that **capitalism and patriarchy are two linked systems**.
Penis envy / womb envy | Freud's claim that girls envy the penis; Horney's reply that **men envy women's power to give birth**.
Écriture féminine | "Women's writing" from the female body and experience (Hélène Cixous).
Gynocriticism | The study of **women writers** (Elaine Showalter; MCQ 2024).
Strategic essentialism | **Temporary unity** around an identity to win political goals (Spivak; MCQ 2026).
Matrix of domination | How **overlapping oppressions** are organised across society (Patricia Hill Collins; MCQ 2026).
Kyriarchy | **Interlocking systems** of domination — gender, race, class, empire (Schüssler Fiorenza; MCQ 2026).
Patriarchal bargain | The **strategies women use** to secure what they can within patriarchy (Kandiyoti; MCQ 2026).
Misogyny | Hatred of, or contempt for, women.
:::

## 0.2  The family tree of feminism

::: fig tree | The family tree of feminist theory: the six syllabus schools and the newer feminisms.
Feminist theory
- Liberal: equal rights through law and education
  - Wollstonecraft
  - Mill
  - Friedan
- Radical: patriarchy is the root
  - Millett
  - Firestone
- Marxist / socialist: capitalism (and patriarchy)
  - Engels
  - Hartmann
- Psychoanalytic: the unconscious mind
  - Horney
  - Chodorow
- Men's feminism: men as allies
  - Connell
  - Kimmel
- Postmodern: no single "woman"
  - Butler
  - Cixous
- Newer feminisms: overlaps and contexts
  - Crenshaw
  - Mohanty
  - Shiva
  - Wadud
:::

## 0.3  The four questions every school answers

The easiest way to keep the schools apart is to ask each of them the same four questions.

::: fig grid | The four questions that separate the schools of feminism.
@cols 4
1 · Cause: why are women disadvantaged?
2 · Where: in law, the family, the economy, the mind, or language?
3 · Remedy: reform the system, or transform it?
4 · Method: laws, protest, revolution, therapy, or new ways of speaking?
:::

::: table | The six syllabus schools on one page.
@widths 2.6,3.8,3.6,4
School | Main cause of women's oppression | Where it happens | Remedy
Liberal | Unequal laws and opportunities | Public life: law, education, work | Reform: equal rights, education, laws
Radical | Patriarchy itself | Everywhere, especially family, sexuality, body | Transform society at the root
Marxist | Capitalism and private property | The economy and class | End capitalism
Socialist | Capitalism **and** patriarchy together | Economy **and** family | Fight both systems
Psychoanalytic | Early childhood and the unconscious | The mind and family | Change childcare; re-read Freud
Men's | Patriarchy harms men too | Masculinities | Men as allies; change manhood
Postmodern | Fixed categories and language | Discourse and identity | Question all categories
:::

::: eye | How the examiner uses this topic
The examiner rarely asks you to list the schools. He or she asks you to **compare** them ("How is radical feminism different from socialist feminism?", 2016), to **explain one in depth** (Marxist/socialist, 2021; psychoanalytic, 2021), to **apply** them to Pakistan ("Which type(s) of feminism can elevate the status of women in Pakistan?", 2018), or to write **short notes** (postmodern, 2019; radical, 2024). Learn each school so that you can (1) define it, (2) name its thinkers and books, (3) state its cause, place and remedy, (4) give its strengths and weaknesses, and (5) apply it to Pakistan.
:::
